'' A CAPTURING LAMBDA WAS REFUSED OUTRIGHT -- "not implemented in this build" -- and it
'' was the largest single feature left, at 32 declines across two sites.
''
'' It is NOT the architectural wall 'spawn' is. fbc's model, measured from its own C,
'' is a UDT with one '__CAP_<NAME>' field per capture -- a POINTER for byref and the
'' value INLINE for byval -- and a '__FBINVOKE' member whose FIRST parameter is the
'' object. NO GENERICS are involved, so the token-range problem that blocks 'spawn'
'' does not apply.
''
'' A BYVAL CAPTURE IS STORED IN THE OBJECT AND MUTATED THERE. Measured by RUNNING it:
'' two calls to a 'sub[ byval n ]' doing 'n += v' print 11 then 12 while the outer n
'' stays 10. So the body must reach the FIELD and not a per-call copy -- which is why
'' the prologue aliases each capture with 'var byref', to the POINTEE for a byref
'' capture and to the FIELD ITSELF for a byval one. With that the body needs no
'' rewriting at all: every mention of a captured name resolves to the alias.
''
'' THE TYPE IS NAMED IN THE PARSER AND BUILT IN RESOLVE, because those are the only two
'' places that can each see half of it: a capture's TYPE is only knowable in the scope
'' the lambda was WRITTEN in, and the type has to live at module level for the hoisted
'' invoke procedure to name it.
''
'' 'c( 5 )' has no operator behind it -- FreeBASIC has no call operator -- so the call
'' is bound by looking for '__fbinvoke' in the OBJECT's type, which is all the call site
'' has. Its receiver is a REAL parameter rather than an implicit THIS, so every
'' parameter query in the argument loop is asked one along.
''
'' generics/lambda-capture.bas: 44 declines to 4. tui/tuiwidget_.bas: 24 to 12.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/lambda-capture.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' GOLDEN: expected/lambda-capture.out
